1
00:00:03,755 --> 00:00:12,885
Stock: item to purchase order to receipt — Operations ▸ Inventory — Set a reorder level once, and the shelf tells you when to order.

2
00:00:18,284 --> 00:00:25,979
Operations ▸ Inventory opens on the numbers that matter: total value, what is low, what is about to expire.

3
00:00:25,979 --> 00:00:44,203
Low Stock Alerts is the working list — two items are below the level someone set for them.

4
00:00:44,203 --> 00:00:52,886
An item needs a name, a type and the unit you actually count it in — boxes, vials, doses.

5
00:00:52,886 --> 00:01:03,155
A category groups it on the overview, and a supplier is who the purchase order goes to.

6
00:01:03,155 --> 00:01:08,760
The reorder level is the one setting that earns its keep — it is what turns a count into an alert.

7
00:01:08,760 --> 00:01:31,904
Set it to what you get through between deliveries, plus a little. Too low and you find out by running out.

8
00:01:31,904 --> 00:01:42,931
Every change is a movement: dispensed, received, adjusted after a count, or written off when it expires.

9
00:01:42,931 --> 00:01:53,639
The stock drops, and the reason stays attached to it. That is what makes a count worth trusting.

10
00:01:53,639 --> 00:02:04,092
Purchase Orders is what you send a supplier. This one is pending — raised against the low flu-vaccine stock.

11
00:02:04,092 --> 00:02:13,597
Approving is the sign-off. Nothing has arrived yet, and the stock has not moved.

12
00:02:13,597 --> 00:02:24,170
Receiving is what raises the stock — one action, and every line on the order lands on the shelf.

13
00:02:24,170 --> 00:02:32,041
Back on the overview the levels have moved and the alert has cleared. Item, order, receipt — that is the whole loop.

14
00:02:32,501 --> 00:02:45,601
What you just did

15
00:02:46,253 --> 00:02:55,253
Health | Efficiency | Growth — More in the Getting Started series — the next video is linked on screen.
